@ARTICLE{X.ISI.000263367800020,
type={inproceedings},
author={Machado, Rodrigo R. and Lourenco, Sergio O.},
title={Nutritional properties of microalgae used as food for bivalve molluscs: a review},
journal={NA},
year={2008},
volume={30},
month={NA},
abstract={Nutritional properties of microalgae used as food for bivalve molluscs: a review. Microalgae used in mariculture are the main source of nutrients for the growth and development of many cultured organisms, such as larvae of bivalve molluscs. The microalgae may show remarkable differences in chemical composition, and this is the most probably explanation for differences in their nutritional quality for several animal species. Important changes in biochemical composition of microalgae occur due to culture conditions, especially as responses to the availability of nutrients and light. Protein tends to occur in higher concentrations when nutrients (especially nitrogen) are abundant, while carbohydrate and lipid increase their concentrations when nutrients decrease. Some specific substances are more important to certain animals than others, making their presence necessary for the preparation of a suitable diet. Deficiency in essential nutrient may decrease the nutritional value of the microalgae and consequently the yield of cultivation of the animal on focus. Differences in nutritional value of microalgae may also be generated by the composition in polysaccharide and due the proportions of essential amino acids, for instance. Levels of polyunsaturated fatty acids (especially eicosapentaenoic acid - 20:5n-3 and docosahexaenoic acid - 22:6n-3), show wide variation among microalgae, and those fatty acids may have a remarkable importance for the determination of the nutritional value of food. Mixed diets tend to be more balanced than monoalgal diets and consequently may promote a better yield in the cultivation of the animals, since the different microalgae offered could compensate for deficiencies that unialgal diets would generate. The selection of microalgal species used in mixed diets must be done taking into account the knowledge of the biochemical composition of the species, as well as the nutritional requirements of the animal. The development of larval culture of bivalves requires a strong qualitative and quantitative control of the food given to the larvae. Research efforts must be done in Brazil to generate original results in this field. Results would be suitable to the nature and specific conditions of Brazil, avoiding the use of information generated in foreign countries, which tend to be of restricted usefulness for the Brazilian reality. The academic sector must offer more subsidies and integrate itself to commercial mariculture, in order to make it possible to achieve the needed development to the cultivation of bivalves in the country.},
