year={2012},
volume={31},
month={NA},
abstract={Two polyculture trials were conducted in a tank to evaluate the mutually beneficial mechanism in the polyculture of kelp Gracilaria lemaneiformis with oyster. In the polyculture, the kelp and the oyster were separately cultured as control groups, and polucultured at low, middle, and high densities. In the first 4-week trial, the oyster were reared at a density of 27 individuals/m(3), and the kelp were cultivated at a density of 0,21,0,21,42,or 84 g/450 L, but 0,71,0,71,142,or 384 g/450 L in the second 4-week trial. During the experiment, the concentrations of NO(3)-N and PO(4)-P, survival and growth of the oyster and kelp were detected every 7 days. It was found that there were significantly lower NO(3)-N and PO(4)-P levels in the tanks with oyster than in the tanks without oyster in the first trial (P<0.05). In the second trial, however, the concentrations of NO(3)-N and PO(4)-P in the tanks with oyster were significantly different from those in the tanks without oyster (P<0.05). There were 43% lower NO(3)-N level, and 30% lower PO(4)-P level in the high density groups than those in the monoculture of the oyster, indicating that the kelp absorbed the nutrients from the co-culture groups at an optimal density of 27 oyster, and 384 g kelp/m(3).},
