month={NA},
abstract={Laboratory-based algal assays were developed to explore the bioavailability of copper (Cu) to the marine alga "Thalassiosira weissflogii". A calibration strategy was developed that avoided the use of the synthetic ligand EDTA in the Aquil growth medium, thereby allowing ambient metal speciation. In a comparison of "T. weissflogii" cells, grown in Aquil medium with EDTA to medium containing no added copper, zinc and < 0.003 nM of EDTA, no significant growth differences were observed after eight days, indicating adequate stored nutrients. A thirty hour assay was selected as the optimal timeframe after examination of data from dose response experiments. Using 5C.U stable isotope additions, parameters examined included growth, chlorophyll a, copper uptake, phytochelatin production and DOC excretion. Specific growth rates of "T. weissflogii" decreased from 1.36/day at pCu = 8.8 to 0.56/day at pCu= 7.8 while intercellular copper concentrations increased from 13.6 fg/cell to 70.1 fg/cell, respectively. Calculated values of IC50 of pCu = 7.7 and copper per algal mass of 625 ug/g were established. Using an algal assay based on EDTA-free culture medium, along with trace metal clean techniques, the effect of copper on "Thalassiosira weissflogii" and the speciation of copper in marine waters can be studied.},
