year={1997},
volume={14},
month={NA},
abstract={Izmir Bay, Turkey, has been polluted gradually since the 1970's with overpopulated city and uncontrolled urbanization. The Bay has become unsuitable for recreational activities as well as ecological deteriorations with colorisation of sea water, local oxygen depletion and the loss of some species. The main problem is eutrophication and therefore, as is well-known to prevent such cases, nutrient inputs must be reduced. However, there is no scientific base to make decision on degree of reduction. Therefore, the scenarios on reducing nutrients needs to be simulated by ecological modelling of lower trophic levels. Eight variables were described such as phytoplankton and zooplankton species, ammonia, nitrate, phosphate, silicate, particulate organic (POC) and dissolved organic matter (DOC). Seven forcing functions have been taken into account such as, cloudness, daylength, daily light intensity at noon, temperature, freshwater input to Inner Bay, external sources of nutrients. The phytoplankton was represented by 9 phytoplankton species. Cell carbon quota values of phytoplankton species, phytoplankton sinking rate, carrying capacity and grazing on phytoplankton were considered. The zooplankton contains copepods, cladocerans, naked ciliates and tintinnid groups. Maximum ingestion rates and carbon quota, respiration rates and assimilation efficiency for different temperatures were gathered from the literature. In the ammonia compartment, primary productivity, zooplankton nitrogen excretion, and nitrification rate from ammonia to nitrate were considered. Phosphate, silicate, particulate organic carbon, dissolved organic carbon compartments were described as mathematical equations of the processes. According to these results a preliminary ecological model of Izmir Bay was created. It was predicted that a 90% reduction in nutrient contents in domestic discharge and rivers would occur by tertiary treatment through this model. Observations seem to consistent with the model results for Nitzschia pungens, Scripsiella trochoidea, Skeletonema costatum partly. In total phytoplankton biomass, comparison between observations and model results showed some deviations in late summer and autumn. Comparison of the model results and data of primary productivity and the phytoplankton biomass shows relatively good agreement. Although this model is quite a preliminary effort for modelling studies for Izmir Bay, it revealed some facts on the lack of data for phytoplankton and zooplankton biomass, kinetic parameters, and temperature dependent variables. The model should be developed to obtain detailed data on these parameters and efforts should be directed to two dimensional models.},
