year={1991},
volume={10},
month={NA},
abstract={The giant scallop Placopecten magellanicus , a benthic suspension-feeder, is exposed to a food supply that fluctuates unpredictably in both quantity and quality. Many species of bivalves are capable of maintaining the flow of energy into growth and reproduction by adjusting the efficiency of feeding or metabolic activity but little is known about the ability of P. magellanicus to exploit its food resource. Mixtures of microalgae and silica were delivered to scallops held in flow through chambers in the laboratory at concentrations ranging from 1 to 12 mg/l and organic fractions ranging from 20 to 90%. The quantity and composition of diets was similar to particle concentrations and ratios of organic to inorganic components actually measured in the natural environment. Scope for growth was estimated for juvenile scallops (40-50 mm shell height) by determining energy gain through feeding and losses associated with respiration and ammonia excretion under these experimental food conditions.},
