journal={Journal of Experimental Marine Biology and Ecology},
year={1983},
volume={73},
month={NA},
abstract={The short-term fate of photosynthetic products within the tissues of the branching coral Stylophora pistillata (Esper) was studied at Eilat, Red Sea. Colonies of S. pistillata were labelled in situ with NaH super(14)CO sub(3) for 24 h and were periodically sampled after 4, 24, 48 h and 1 month. Amounts of super(14)C products were measured in the upper parts of the branches (up to 2.5 cm below the tips). Branches were divided into different fragments: branch-tips, mid-branches, and bases. All fragments below a branch-tip contain significantly more super(14)C products than the tip (the upper 0.5 cm), while no significant differences were recorded between all the other fragments. The retention rate in super(14)C materials in tips and bases within the same colony is approximately equal. This and the result that no gradual increase of super(14)C products in the tissue of the tips is recorded, exclude the possibility of significant translocation of super(14)C materials from bases to tips during the first month after incubation. It is suggested that the marked loss of photosynthetic products (up to 80%) is the result of a combination of several processes such as production of mucus, larval release, tissue growth, and metabolic requirements.},
