month={NA},
abstract={Dense animals assemblages consisting of vestimentiferan worms, branchyuran crabs and giant clams have been found clustered around deep-sea hydrothermal vents in unusual ecosystems that appear to be independent of photosynthetically produced nitrients. Reduced compounds such as H sub(2)S, H sub(2) and CH sub(4) are spewed from the vents into the cold, oxygenated bottom water, and it has been suggested that chemoautrotrophic bacteria are important primary producers of organic nutrients utilized by the vent fauna. In some instances, sulphur-oxidizing bacteria may perform this function through growth as symbionts within tissues of host animals (clams and vestimentiferans). To gain further insight into sulphur metabolism in these unusual food webs, the authors have analysed the stable sulphur isotope ratios ( super(34)S/ super(32)S) of Pacific vent fauna and find delta super(34)S values close to 0 ppt. These values approximate the +1 to +4ppt range observed for sulfur-bearing mineral at the vents, and indicate that this specialized fauna utilizes sulfur derived from the vent (rather than from seawater sulphate) during growth and metabolism.},
