abstract={Ratios of GDH activity: NH sub(4) super(+) excretion and ETS activity: oxygen consumption were measured in western Gulf of Mexico zooplankton and averaged 18.7 plus or minus 4.3 and 2.65 plus or minus 0.55, respectively. These ratios were used to estimate NH sub(4) super(+) excretion and respiration rates of two natural zooplankton assemblages sampled quantitatively for GDH and ETS activity with a Multiple Opening and Closing Net and Environmental Sensing System (MOCNESS). Greater than 80% of the total GDH and ETS activity 0-200 m was concentrated in depth strata above the chlorophyll maximum, suggesting a strong zooplankton-phytoplankton grazing interaction. GDH activity per unit of zooplankton protein biomass was similar to 3-fold greater in the upper 100 m than between 100-200 m, while ETS activity per unit of zooplankton protein biomass showed no consistent pattern with increasing depth. O:N metabolic quotients were estimated for the zooplankton sampled with the MOCNESS by ratioing GDH-excretion and ETS-respiratory by atoms.},
