abstract={Eggs and larvae of the winter flounder P. americanus were hatched and raised in the laboratory under controlled conditions. Biochemical composition was measured during development and found to be similar to that of other species: 65 to 80 percent protein, 15 to 30 percent fat, and 0 to 5 percent carbohydrate. Respiration rates of eggs were low, but rose gradually from fertilization to hatching. Respiration rates of early larvae were from two to eight times that of eggs. Variation in larval respiration rates indicated a three-fold difference in rate according to level of activity. Eggs excreted ammonial at an increasing rate from fertilization to hatching. Larvae excreted ammonia, primary amines, and other unidentified organic nitrogenous substances. Rates of excretion and proportions of excretory products varied with stage of development.},
