year={1982},
volume={1},
month={NA},
abstract={Ammonium (NH super(+)d4) is a preferred nitrogen source for Antarctic phytoplankton growth and the principal nitrogenous waste excreted by zooplankton and nicronekton. In austral summer 1977-1978, NH super(+)d4 was present at average concentrations of 0.1-0.3 mu g-at liter super(-1) in the upper 50 m of the Ross Sea and was excreted by resident mixed zooplankton at rates of 1.6 mu g-at NH sub(4)u+ g super(-1) wet weight h super(-1). Zooplankton biomass sampled by vertically-towed 0.5 m nets averaged 0.06 ml m super(-3) in the upper 200 m over the Ross Sea shelf and 0.09 ml m super(-3) in the upper 200 m north of the shelf-slope front. Olson (1980) has reported that phytoplankton nitrogenous requirements were 110 and 170 mu g-at NH sub(4)u+ m super(-3) day super(-1) in these respective regions. A synthesis of these data predicts that average summertime standing crops of zooplankton might regenerate only about 2% of the daily NH sub(4)u+ requirements of Ross Sea phytoplankton.},
