year={1980},
volume={NA},
month={NA},
abstract={Animal waste from sheep and rabbit was used as a nitrogen source for culturing G. tikvahiae and N. baileyi (Rhodophyceae) at 23 C, 56 Ein /m/s for 6 days. The culture media ranged from 0.008 to 0.08% dry weight animal waste. Optimal growth appeared between 0.04 and 0.08%. Sheep waste supported optimal growth of 0.19 and 0.18 g/g in N. baileyi and G. tikvahiae , respectively. Rabbit waste on the other hand supported a higher optimal growth of 0.45 and 0.28 g/g in N. baileyi and G. tikvahiae , respectively. Recommended concentrations of Provasoli's enrichment supported G. tikvahiae growth of 0.12 g/g during the experimental period. These results suggest a potential for the utilization of sheep and rabbit waste as a nutrient source for growing these economically important red algae. Although sheep waste had a higher inorganic nitrogen content than rabbit waste, the latter supported a better growth (1.5-2 times the optimal growth observed in the algae using sheep waste). This result was attributed to the greater turbidity produced from sheep waste, reducing the light reaching the algae in the culture dishes.},
