month={NA},
abstract={The authors report the isolation of a chlorate-resistant mutant of N.muscorum defective in nitrate metabolism and sharing nitrate inhibition of growth in nitrogen-free medium with no significant effect on heterocyst differentiation and frequency. Results show that algae grow better with different concns of nitrate or nitrite than with mol nitrogen, and its growth with 5 mM chlorate in combined nitrogen-free medium is almost nonexistent. Since nitrate-grown cultures contain preformed nitrate reductase, and since such cultures form heterocysts in the presence of chlorate, the substrate (NO Sub(3)) analogue of the enzyme, the authors consider that formation of nitrate reductase and heterocyst are not mutually exclusive and that nitrate does not directly inhibit the formation of heterocysts. Growth toxicity of 5 mM chlorate is decreased to a significant level by 5 mM nitrate, and the growth protective action of nitrate against chlorate increases with nitrate concn. Increasing concns of chlorate counteract the growth-promoting actions of nitrate; and it appears that chlorate and nitrate act as a pair of competitive antagonists in this alga. The chlorate resistant strain Clr-1 is one of a series of mutants that is more resistant to chlorate than the parent strain, but is not completely insensitive. From their results, the authors conclude the Clr-1 lacks active nitrate reductase. The growth response of Clr-1 to chlorate and nitrate under nitrogen fixing conditions indicates that it is permeable to nitrate and chlorate which inhibit its growth under nitrogen-fixing conditions by inhibiting the activity of nitrogenase enzyme with no effect on heterocyst formation. This implies that active nitrogenase does not regulate heteocyst formation or heterocyst spacing pattern in N.muscorum.},
