abstract={Anatomical, biochemical, and metabolic characteristics of Atlantic copepods have been assessed during the summer upwelling off the coast of Morocco. The respiratory rate increases between 13 degrees and 2 degrees C, with a Q sub(l0) varying from 1.8 to 5.3. At temperatures higher than 20 degrees C there seems to be regulation in Acartia clausi Giesbrecht and Centropages typicus Kroyer, both abundant in warm waters, but not for other spp whose respiration rate decreases. Respiration may be expressed as a function of temp and dry weight as: R = 1.755.1.097 super(T).W super(l.0l9), with a mean Q sub(l0) of 2.47 for planktonic copepods during the cruise. The copepods excrete approx 14.13 per cent of nitrogen per day, the Q sub(10) of the excretion rates being low. Temora stylifera (Dana) has been further investigated with regard to the effect of starvation on metabolism. Respiration, excretion and specific excretion immediately decrease; the oxygen/nitrogen ratio decreases after 4 days, and after one week starvation the copepod survives on proteins exclusively. Calanus helgolandicus (Claus) has been studied in view of the energetic aspects of its vertical migration. It is shown that, depending on the ingestion rate, phytoplankton biomass, and temp the vertical migration will either be energetically rewarding, with an optimal time in the upper layer for the largest profit or unfavourable; in the latter case it might be suitable for the organism to adapt its metabolism to conditions at depth in order to survive.},
